<p>You open a lettuce that still feels fairly firm and notice red, pink or brown edges on some leaves. It is easy to interpret any colour change as proof that the product can no longer be eaten, but leafy vegetables are more complicated than that. <strong>Colour alone cannot tell you whether lettuce is spoiled.</strong></p>
<p>Leaves remain living plant tissues after harvest. They continue to respire, lose water and react to cuts, bruises and storage conditions. Ageing, oxidation of natural phenolic compounds, dehydration and physiological damage can all alter colour before true rot develops.</p>
<h2>Why lettuce develops red or brown edges</h2>
<p>When a leaf is cut, bent, bruised or simply ages, cells are damaged and substances that were previously separated inside the tissue can interact. Enzymatic and oxidative reactions may then produce pink, red or brown colours.</p>
<p>Leaf margins are also particularly exposed to the surrounding air and can lose water faster than central tissue. A dry brown edge may therefore reflect ageing and dehydration rather than microbial spoilage.</p>
<h2>Judge colour together with texture, smell and moisture</h2>
<p>The most useful assessment combines several observations. A leaf with one dry brown tip that remains crisp, dry to the touch and normally scented is very different from a leaf that is slimy, translucent, wet and unpleasant smelling.</p>
<p>We recommend focusing on four factors: <strong>firmness, surface moisture, smell and extent of damage</strong>. Colour provides information, but should not decide the outcome on its own.</p>
<h2>When the problem is mainly loss of quality</h2>
<ul>
<li>Limited dry, red or brown margins.</li>
<li>Leaves that still retain structure and some crispness.</li>
<li>No slimy surface.</li>
<li>Normal fresh vegetal smell.</li>
<li>No visible mould or spreading watery lesions.</li>
</ul>
<p>Under these conditions, the change mainly affects presentation and texture. You can trim less attractive margins and use the rest of the leaf if it remains sound. A whole lettuce does not need to be discarded because of a few dry tips.</p>
<h2>When to discard lettuce</h2>
<p>The decision changes when you find <strong>slime, wet soft lesions, abnormal odours, mould, collapsed tissue or widespread breakdown</strong>. A leaf that falls apart between your fingers and has a slippery film is not simply dehydrated.</p>
<p>Be particularly conservative when wet deterioration extends through many inner leaves. Once decay is widespread, selecting a few apparently clean pieces is not a sensible way to rescue the product.</p>
<h2>Dry browning versus a wet lesion</h2>
<p>This distinction is very practical. A dry edge tends to feel thin, papery or brittle and is often clearly defined. A wet spoilage lesion is more likely to be soft, translucent, sunken or gelatinous and can spread into neighbouring tissue.</p>
<p>Free surface water changes the environment around a leaf. This is why lettuce stored wet and compressed for days can deteriorate differently from lettuce that has simply wilted through water loss.</p>
<h2>Why outer leaves age first</h2>
<p>Outer leaves are more exposed to air, handling, pressure and changes in humidity during harvest, transport and storage. They are therefore often the first to lose turgor or show small damaged areas.</p>
<p>When lettuce arrives at home, inspect these leaves and plan to use the most delicate ones first. Removing one damaged but dry outer leaf does not mean the heart of the lettuce is spoiled.</p>
<h2>Cutting accelerates browning</h2>
<p>A whole lettuce does not age in the same way as pre-cut leaves. Cutting creates a much larger area of injured tissue and exposes internal cells to oxygen. Browning reactions and water loss therefore accelerate.</p>
<p>If you prepare lettuce ahead of time, use clean utensils, refrigerate it promptly and avoid unnecessary handling. Cutting only what you need can preserve the remaining head more effectively.</p>
<h2>Should lettuce be washed before storage?</h2>
<p>It can be, but thorough drying becomes essential. Wet leaves packed tightly together create areas where water remains trapped for hours, encouraging rapid deterioration.</p>
<p>Many households find it easier to store lettuce dry and wash only the portion needed. If you wash in advance, a salad spinner and a clean container that does not trap excessive condensation are useful.</p>
<h2>How to store lettuce to slow quality loss</h2>
<p>Lettuce generally benefits from refrigeration and relatively high humidity to reduce dehydration. A vegetable crisper drawer often provides a more suitable environment than a very dry open shelf.</p>
<p>The aim is to maintain humid air without leaving the leaves soaked. Protect the lettuce from heavy foods that can crush it. Our guide to <a href="/en/how-to-store-vegetables-what-goes-in-the-fridge-and-what-does-not/">storing vegetables properly</a> explains the wider principles.</p>
<h2>Can slightly wilted lettuce be revived?</h2>
<p>A sound leaf that has lost water may recover some firmness after contact with cold water. This can improve texture, but <strong>it does not reverse ageing and cannot make spoiled tissue safe</strong>.</p>
<p>If there is slime, mould or an unpleasant smell, soaking is not a solution. Rehydration only makes sense for healthy leaves that have lost turgor.</p>
<h2>Red does not automatically mean “bad”</h2>
<p>Online advice sometimes treats any red edge as proof that lettuce should be thrown away. That rule is too simplistic. Pigmentation and browning can be normal tissue responses to cutting, stress and ageing.</p>
<p>The better question is how the tissue behaves. Firm leaves without slime, mould or abnormal odours should be interpreted differently from wet, collapsing leaves.</p>
<h2>What to do when several areas look affected</h2>
<p>Open the head and inspect more than one layer. If the issue is limited to dry outer tips, remove them and prioritise the lettuce for early consumption. If wet lesions repeat from the outside towards the heart, the pattern suggests more advanced deterioration.</p>
<p>This layered inspection is useful because the exterior does not always reveal what is happening inside. You do not need to dismantle a sound lettuce, but checking the centre makes sense when the outside raises genuine doubts.</p>
<h2>A practical rule</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Limited dry red or brown edge:</strong> check texture and smell; usually quality loss.</li>
<li><strong>Wilted but dry leaf:</strong> mainly water loss; use promptly if otherwise sound.</li>
<li><strong>Slime, abnormal wetness, bad smell or mould:</strong> discard affected material and assess the whole lettuce conservatively.</li>
<li><strong>Widespread wet deterioration:</strong> do not try to salvage tiny apparently healthy areas.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Can I trim brown edges and eat the rest?</h3>
<p>Yes when damage is dry and limited and the remaining leaf has normal texture and smell. Trimming is not an appropriate way to rescue mouldy or wet-rotten tissue.</p>
<h3>Is slightly wilted lettuce unsafe?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily. Wilting mainly indicates water loss. Safety depends on storage history and genuine spoilage signs.</p>
<h3>Does refrigeration prevent browning completely?</h3>
<p>No. Cold slows many processes, but leaves continue to respire and age and can still dehydrate, oxidise or suffer physical damage.</p>
<h3>Does a red edge mean the lettuce tastes worse?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily. A dry aged margin may be less pleasant in texture, but flavour cannot be predicted from colour alone.</p>
